    "body": "Unfortunately, the ZSH init still causes a prompt draw per execution on my system. You can check this by starting a continuation prompt, then changing the config. If the continuation prompt changes immediately without needing to restart the shell, starship is re-evaluating the character every time:\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/4605384/147704957-4d6feb19-7cbc-4934-8381-530e3d88d2f3.png)\r\n\r\n\r\nA general question about the design/positioning of this: my impression of this is that this is a convenience function for starship users, i.e. it wouldn't be hard to set this yourself, but we're providing a set of shell wrappers to set simple continuation prompts for them.\r\n\r\nGiven this, does it make sense for this to be in the character module, which usually deals with displaying dynamic information (e.g. command success, vi-mode, etc)? I wonder if it's a little misleading, because it implies that the module responds to stuff that the character module usually responds to.",
    "state": "CHANGES_REQUESTED",
